WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
Something surprising just happened in the world of AI.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.120
A company known for turning your words into pictures

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.540
just revealed its first real piece of hardware.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.720
Instead of another art tool, mid-journey

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.820
built a full-body scanner that uses rings of sensors

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.360
and sound waves to create detailed images inside your body.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.460
Think of it like taking the clever pattern spotting

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.520
that makes pretty art and turning it into a device

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.080
that can spot health issues.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.540
The scanner creates a vertical slice

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.620
as you stand inside the ring, giving a quick, non-invasive view

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.720
without needing a big hospital machine.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.420
This shift matters because AI tools

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.300
are no longer stuck on your screen.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.340
They are starting to touch real things like health and daily life.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.000
For a student curious about medicine

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.360
or a parent thinking about future checkups,

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.840
it hints that scans could one day happen at a local spot

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.580
instead of only in clinics.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.520
It also shows how companies that started with creative fun

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.620
might help solve practical problems

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.760
like reducing strain on medical systems.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.420
If this direction works, it could make proactive health checks

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.280
feel more normal and less scary.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.640
Right now, there is no public demo you can try,

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.180
but the announcement itself is worth watching

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.880
because it shows one company's surprising next step

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.620
from pictures to physical tools.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.320
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.620
The Deep Dive, where we look under the hood

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.760
and figure out how something actually works.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.740
Today, we are unpacking the city building game idea

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.960
that explains the mid-journey scanner.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.080
You know how when you are playing a city building game,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.960
you place roads and buildings one piece at a time

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.700
and suddenly the whole town feels alive and connected?

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.840
Now imagine the game gets much smarter.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.440
Instead of you placing every single road,

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.900
the game starts guessing the best next piece

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.820
based on everything already built.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.580
And it keeps doing that over and over

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.620
until a full working system appears.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.880
Then picture swapping the roads for sound waves

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.140
bouncing through a body.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.540
The system predicts the clearest path for each wave,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.920
fills in the blanks and builds a complete inner picture

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.920
without anyone drawing it by hand.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.980
And that is basically what the new mid-journey scanner is doing

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.880
when it turns simple sensor readings

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.460
into a full body view.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.620
It takes the same step-by-step prediction trick

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.840
that makes image generators work

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.600
and applies it to real world signals.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.080
So next time someone says ultra sound hardware

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.540
from an AI company, you can tell them

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.120
it is basically a city builder that works with sound

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.060
instead of pixels.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:33.820
Not so scary, right?

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.960
Now let's move into cool stuff and try this.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.260
First up, a free way to build a whole website

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.880
just by chatting with an AI.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.000
A template pack lets you point any chatbot

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.980
at a folder of ready-made designs

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.740
and describe exactly what kind of site you want.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.800
The AI looks at the templates,

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.220
asks you a couple of quick questions about colors or style,

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.480
then builds a working page for you.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.220
It is perfect if you have ever wanted a cool online portfolio

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:04.360
or project page, but do not know how to code.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.460
Grab the free template pack, download the folder,

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.840
then open any chatbot like chat GPT or Claude

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.480
and type exactly this.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.080
Make me a site about my favorite game

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.900
using the templates in this folder.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.220
The chatbot will scan the designs

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.220
and create something you can view in your browser right away.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.780
No coding needed, just chat and watch it appear.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.780
Here is another fun one you can try right now.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:31.600
A simple online gallery lets you watch

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.300
how 20 different AI systems each create

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.020
a three-dimensional harbor town

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.500
that grows from ancient times all the way to the future.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.720
You can click through the versions,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.400
pause on the ones you like,

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.020
and even control the camera to explore the town.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.240
It is a fun way to compare how creative different

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.060
AI's can be when given the exact same request.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.300
Head to the gallery site on your phone or laptop

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.240
and just start clicking through the scenes.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.660
No signup required and you will see how each model

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.140
handles the same idea in its own style.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.040
Time for quick bits.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:08.360
The company called Waymo has decided to recall

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.520
more than 3,800 of its robot axes.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.420
A software issue is being addressed by the team

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.220
at this self-driving taxi firm.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.880
This issue occasionally caused the vehicles

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.100
to attempt entering areas of freeway construction

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:23.240
that were closed off.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.380
The action highlights the importance

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.120
of ongoing checks for the fleet.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.600
Even the most sophisticated systems

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.620
for autonomous driving require thorough evaluation.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.480
Real world conditions can present unexpected challenges

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.300
at any moment.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.040
Testing remains essential before wider deployment proceeds.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.060
Here is another quick one.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.300
A new poll found that 63% of people think

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.000
the technology is advancing quicker than feels comfortable.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.640
The survey comes from Pew Research

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.740
and covers attitudes toward AI.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.960
More of us are using chatbots than ever before

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.540
according to the same data.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.860
The results show excitement and caution growing

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:02.780
at the same time.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.200
63% specifically noted the pace feels

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:07.420
faster than comfortable.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.020
The findings were reported by the Verge

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.140
drawing directly from the poll numbers.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:16.240
This mix of views appears even as daily chatbot usage

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.900
continues to rise, and that is a wrap.

